Fix reserve-job WAL persistence (issue #61; upstream bug, unfixed in
v1.13 and on master).

When persistence is enabled (-b <dir>), reserving a buried or delayed job via
`reserve-job <id>` transitions the job to Reserved in memory (and subsequently
to Ready upon release with delay 0, worker disconnection, or TTR timeout),
but OP_RESERVE_JOB previously failed to update the Write-Ahead Log (WAL) or
reserve WAL space.

Because return-to-ready operations (OP_RELEASE with delay 0, conn_close, and
conn_timeout) bypass walwrite() under the assumption that reserved jobs
already exist as Ready on disk, the on-disk binlog permanently retained the
stale Buried or Delayed state record. Upon daemon restart or crash recovery,
prot_replay replayed the binlog and restored the job back to Buried or Delayed,
removing active work from the queue. Furthermore, if the daemon restarted
while a worker held an in-flight reservation of a previously buried job, the
job recovered as Buried rather than Ready.

When reserving a job currently in Buried or Delayed state, reserve WAL space
via walresvupdate(&c->srv->wal) before modifying queue state, and write the
updated job record to the WAL via walwrite(&c->srv->wal, j) followed by
walmaint(&c->srv->wal). The record is written with state Reserved, which
readrec() converts to Ready during binlog replay, ensuring the job recovers
as Ready across daemon restarts.

--- a/prot.c
+++ b/prot.c
@@ -1488,6 +1488,16 @@
             return;
         }
 
+        int should_update_wal = (j->r.state == Buried || j->r.state == Delayed);
+        if (should_update_wal) {
+            int z = walresvupdate(&c->srv->wal);
+            if (!z) {
+                reply_serr(c, MSG_OUT_OF_MEMORY);
+                return;
+            }
+            j->walresv += z;
+        }
+
         // Job can be in ready, buried or delayed states.
         if (j->r.state == Ready) {
             j = remove_ready_job(j);
@@ -1505,6 +1515,14 @@
         global_stat.reserved_ct++;
 
         conn_reserve_job(c, j);
+
+        if (should_update_wal) {
+            if (!walwrite(&c->srv->wal, j)) {
+                reply_serr(c, MSG_INTERNAL_ERROR);
+                return;
+            }
+            walmaint(&c->srv->wal);
+        }
         reply_job(c, j, MSG_RESERVED);
         return;
 
